[SPEAKER_01]: I had a great picture of him.

643
00:28:49,754 --> 00:28:50,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Groot and Mays.

644
00:28:51,375 --> 00:28:52,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Ernie Shore.

645
00:28:52,776 --> 00:28:53,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

646
00:28:53,617 --> 00:28:55,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Keith Gedeon and Joe Wood.

647
00:28:57,524 --> 00:29:00,805
[SPEAKER_01]: He lost his pitching line.

648
00:29:00,985 --> 00:29:05,106
[SPEAKER_02]: He lost his pitching line.

649
00:29:05,706 --> 00:29:11,047
[SPEAKER_02]: He talked to Ernie Schorr down in North Carolina because he had a perfect game, you know.

650
00:29:12,007 --> 00:29:16,668
[SPEAKER_02]: But they had a good pitching staff, the Red Sox.

651
00:29:17,448 --> 00:29:18,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Crazy, he showed them all.

652
00:29:23,229 --> 00:29:25,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, how about after baseball, what did you do?

653
00:29:26,890 --> 00:29:33,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I went, uh, I coached at St. Mary's, coached baseball there for a number of years.

654
00:29:35,254 --> 00:29:41,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I operated the student store, bookstore, and, you know, odds and ends.

655
00:29:41,217 --> 00:29:43,858
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I kept me pretty busy.

656
00:29:44,979 --> 00:29:46,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I retired in about 1960.

657
00:29:47,800 --> 00:29:51,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, uh, where you live, uh, how far away is St. Mary's?

658
00:29:53,083 --> 00:29:55,164
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, uh, what's about 40 to 45 miles.

659
00:29:56,610 --> 00:29:57,270
[SPEAKER_01]: in Milwaukee.

660
00:29:58,791 --> 00:30:03,492
[SPEAKER_01]: From San Francisco it would be about 21 miles.

661
00:30:03,672 --> 00:30:07,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a beautiful place.

662
00:30:07,313 --> 00:30:08,713
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all built up.

663
00:30:09,333 --> 00:30:14,714
[SPEAKER_01]: When we were out there, the pioneers went out there, the colonels went out there and moved from Oakland in 1929.

664
00:30:14,734 --> 00:30:15,715
[SPEAKER_01]: There was nothing else.

665
00:30:27,478 --> 00:30:28,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Petaluma?

666
00:30:28,278 --> 00:30:28,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Petaluma.

667
00:30:29,239 --> 00:30:30,880
[SPEAKER_02]: Petaluma, California.

668
00:30:30,900 --> 00:30:31,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Petaluma.

669
00:30:31,540 --> 00:30:31,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

670
00:30:31,901 --> 00:30:32,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Petaluma.

671
00:30:32,921 --> 00:30:33,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Petaluma.

672
00:30:33,902 --> 00:30:35,403
[SPEAKER_02]: I put an N in it, I think.

673
00:30:37,184 --> 00:30:41,546
[SPEAKER_02]: This is a little bit north of San Francisco.

674
00:30:43,047 --> 00:30:47,890
[SPEAKER_02]: K08 campground here on Route 101 or something like it.

675
00:30:49,711 --> 00:30:52,072
[SPEAKER_02]: On Saturday night, June 24th.

676
00:30:53,173 --> 00:30:56,715
[SPEAKER_02]: We had this interview with Louis Guisto earlier today.

677
00:30:58,288 --> 00:31:00,810
[SPEAKER_02]: It was not scheduled, really.

678
00:31:01,690 --> 00:31:07,334
[SPEAKER_02]: We spent last night at Lodi, which is about 110 miles away, I guess.

679
00:31:09,175 --> 00:31:14,679
[SPEAKER_02]: And we drove on over this morning on a lovely day.

680
00:31:15,179 --> 00:31:19,782
[SPEAKER_02]: And we thought we'd try Napa, where Guisto lives.

681
00:31:20,743 --> 00:31:26,347
[SPEAKER_02]: And if not Napa, then up to Boys Springs, where Penelli lives, or Glen Ellen, where Mark Koenig lives.

682
00:31:28,813 --> 00:31:35,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Then we thought we'd maybe come over here after that or else come over here before that and go back and make the interviews later.

683
00:31:35,878 --> 00:31:37,739
[SPEAKER_02]: We weren't quite sure what the plan would be.

684
00:31:39,120 --> 00:31:43,843
[SPEAKER_02]: But we got into Napa and got out to Guisto's house about quarter of 11.

685
00:31:44,944 --> 00:31:45,844
[SPEAKER_02]: And he wasn't there.

686
00:31:45,884 --> 00:31:46,965
[SPEAKER_02]: The garage was empty.

687
00:31:48,246 --> 00:31:50,187
[SPEAKER_02]: And the spray was going in the front lawn.

688
00:31:51,268 --> 00:31:52,348
[SPEAKER_02]: I went up and knocked on the door.

689
00:31:52,368 --> 00:31:52,909
[SPEAKER_02]: No answer.

690
00:31:52,929 --> 00:31:57,232
[SPEAKER_02]: A gentleman from across the street came over and said that he saw that we were from West Virginia.

691
00:31:58,407 --> 00:32:01,688
[SPEAKER_02]: make sure we knew what the story was.

692
00:32:01,808 --> 00:32:07,090
[SPEAKER_02]: So he said Guisto had gone out shortly before and should be back very soon.

693
00:32:07,690 --> 00:32:14,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Even though he was in his 80s, he was in good shape physically and would be disappointed if we hadn't seen him.

694
00:32:14,732 --> 00:32:15,473
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know about that.

695
00:32:16,913 --> 00:32:18,014
[SPEAKER_02]: So we thought about it.

696
00:32:18,034 --> 00:32:19,774
[SPEAKER_02]: Then we went into town and got something to eat.

697
00:32:19,794 --> 00:32:20,574
[SPEAKER_02]: We hadn't eaten much.

698
00:32:20,634 --> 00:32:25,896
[SPEAKER_02]: We went into the butter, not dairy, butterflower bakery, I guess it was.

699
00:32:25,916 --> 00:32:28,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Had some coffee and donuts.

700
00:32:30,129 --> 00:32:31,189
[SPEAKER_02]: Then came back out.

701
00:32:31,209 --> 00:32:32,729
[SPEAKER_02]: It was about 11.30 now.

702
00:32:33,690 --> 00:32:35,950
[SPEAKER_02]: I talked to somebody else who said he should be back.

703
00:32:36,870 --> 00:32:37,910
[SPEAKER_02]: We waited out in the car.

704
00:32:37,950 --> 00:32:39,751
[SPEAKER_02]: We were going to leave after about 10 minutes.

705
00:32:39,771 --> 00:32:41,071
[SPEAKER_02]: But he pulled in about then.

706
00:32:42,651 --> 00:32:50,853
[SPEAKER_02]: And the gentleman next door came rushing out to tell him that there were some visitors from West Virginia, which didn't seem to thrill him greatly.

707
00:32:50,933 --> 00:32:54,273
[SPEAKER_02]: But he acknowledged us and invited us in.

708
00:32:55,593 --> 00:32:56,814
[SPEAKER_02]: And he was kind of rushed.

709
00:32:56,834 --> 00:32:58,094
[SPEAKER_02]: He's a very ill sister.

710
00:32:58,154 --> 00:32:59,494
[SPEAKER_02]: And he had other things to do.

711
00:33:03,034 --> 00:33:04,554
[SPEAKER_02]: But he could give us a little bit of time.

712
00:33:04,674 --> 00:33:10,195
[SPEAKER_02]: So we sat down there about 11.30 and had the half hour tape, the other side of this tape.

713
00:33:10,215 --> 00:33:13,976
[SPEAKER_02]: It wasn't much to it, but at least I talked to him and I heard him.

714
00:33:13,996 --> 00:33:17,077
[SPEAKER_02]: He signed the ball, and we got a couple pictures.

715
00:33:18,297 --> 00:33:20,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, could you describe the house?

716
00:33:22,479 --> 00:33:27,780
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a stone house with a little

717
00:33:28,581 --> 00:33:39,506
[SPEAKER_00]: porch-like affair coming out in front, a veranda-type thing that was covered with ivy that was thicker than your arm, the stalks.

718
00:33:39,726 --> 00:33:44,308
[SPEAKER_02]: He said you could take off the porch pillars, and the ivy stalks would hold up the roof.

719
00:33:44,868 --> 00:33:45,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

720
00:33:46,068 --> 00:33:50,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And inside the house, it was very nicely furnished, actually.

721
00:33:50,470 --> 00:33:54,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Not modern, by any means, but traditional furniture.

722
00:33:54,902 --> 00:33:57,464
[SPEAKER_00]: and pictures all over of nieces and nephews.

723
00:33:57,544 --> 00:34:01,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he has 15 nephews living in Napa alone.

724
00:34:02,387 --> 00:34:09,171
[SPEAKER_00]: In the dining room, it was a traditional dining room thing with a lace tablecloth on it, a plastic tablecloth on top of that.

725
00:34:09,912 --> 00:34:13,874
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sideboard, or the buffet, was loaded with liquor bottles.

726
00:34:15,696 --> 00:34:19,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And so were all the ashtrays loaded with, they had all been used.

727
00:34:19,738 --> 00:34:22,400
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that little kitchen or den, whatever it was,

728
00:34:22,894 --> 00:34:25,735
[SPEAKER_00]: was piled high with papers and books.

729
00:34:25,755 --> 00:34:29,636
[SPEAKER_00]: It looked like your type of journal, whatever was in it.

730
00:34:29,656 --> 00:34:33,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe desk bookkeeping on the side or something.

731
00:34:33,677 --> 00:34:35,797
[SPEAKER_02]: A little bit of clutter out there in the back rooms.

732
00:34:36,437 --> 00:34:37,558
[SPEAKER_00]: But not in the living room.

733
00:34:37,638 --> 00:34:41,058
[SPEAKER_00]: It was except for the pictures, which were all over Kingdom Come.

734
00:34:41,599 --> 00:34:47,960
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, they must be Catholic, because they have a Madonna and child up there and a crucifix.

735
00:34:49,861 --> 00:34:51,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And has no children of his own.

736
00:34:52,273 --> 00:34:54,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether he was married or not, we never did find out.

737
00:34:55,694 --> 00:35:00,696
[SPEAKER_00]: But we know one younger sister and one older sister.

738
00:35:01,357 --> 00:35:05,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And the younger sister has six grandchildren or something.

739
00:35:05,598 --> 00:35:11,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he said he had 15 to 18 nephews in the valley in Napa.

740
00:35:14,982 --> 00:35:19,124
[SPEAKER_02]: The front of the house is covered with bushes and trees and shrubbery.

741
00:35:19,144 --> 00:35:20,264
[SPEAKER_02]: You can hardly see the house.

742
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:29,524
[SPEAKER_02]: And I walked around the back where this other gentleman spoke to me, and he had looked rather cluttered in the back with bushes and trees.

743
00:35:29,624 --> 00:35:30,985
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't see any lawn or anything.

744
00:35:31,765 --> 00:35:38,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe he's got an orchard and some vines of his own out there.

745
00:35:38,328 --> 00:35:41,970
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a very good health for his age.

746
00:35:42,030 --> 00:35:43,130
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't look 83.

747
00:35:43,391 --> 00:35:47,172
[SPEAKER_02]: He's a little hunched over, very stouty, always a stout.

748
00:35:47,833 --> 00:35:48,413
[SPEAKER_02]: Gray hair.

749
00:35:49,575 --> 00:35:52,377
[SPEAKER_02]: Rather firm features and a firm manner.

750
00:35:54,818 --> 00:35:57,680
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember his pictures back in the 20s with a mustache.

751
00:35:57,700 --> 00:36:00,102
[SPEAKER_02]: He seemed so unusual wearing a mustache then.

752
00:36:00,882 --> 00:36:02,083
[SPEAKER_02]: I think I did.

753
00:36:02,103 --> 00:36:03,104
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't have one now.

754
00:36:03,804 --> 00:36:07,106
[SPEAKER_02]: But he still was recognizable, though, as I recall those old pictures.

755
00:36:10,108 --> 00:36:12,670
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't even tell any good stories after we left.

756
00:36:12,970 --> 00:36:14,351
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, after the tape went off.

757
00:36:15,592 --> 00:36:18,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think he was surprised that you left as soon as it switched off.

758
00:36:19,777 --> 00:36:20,917
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you do?

759
00:36:20,977 --> 00:36:21,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

760
00:36:21,618 --> 00:36:22,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

761
00:36:22,058 --> 00:36:26,119
[SPEAKER_00]: I just got that impression that he was surprised you were ready to leave.

762
00:36:27,300 --> 00:36:29,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I felt that he wanted me to leave.

763
00:36:30,341 --> 00:36:32,682
[SPEAKER_02]: There was kind of an imposition coming in like that.

764
00:36:32,762 --> 00:36:36,183
[SPEAKER_02]: And I said, do you have some time?

765
00:36:36,203 --> 00:36:37,263
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, a few minutes.

766
00:36:37,343 --> 00:36:39,184
[SPEAKER_02]: And I said, well, he said, how much time?

767
00:36:39,204 --> 00:36:39,984
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, maybe an hour.

768
00:36:40,004 --> 00:36:41,765
[SPEAKER_02]: And he looked at his watch and said, well, just an hour.

769
00:36:43,145 --> 00:36:47,887
[SPEAKER_02]: So from that clue, I felt imposed to take a half an hour.

770
00:36:49,484 --> 00:36:53,946
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't think he was unhappy to quit then.

771
00:36:56,788 --> 00:36:58,149
[SPEAKER_02]: The living room was dark.

772
00:36:58,169 --> 00:36:59,590
[SPEAKER_02]: There were no lights on in there.

773
00:37:00,390 --> 00:37:05,173
[SPEAKER_02]: I thought we might take some – we did take a couple pictures.

774
00:37:05,273 --> 00:37:09,195
[SPEAKER_02]: I hope they came out, but there wasn't much.

775
00:37:09,255 --> 00:37:10,576
[SPEAKER_02]: It was kind of gloomy in a way.

776
00:37:11,016 --> 00:37:13,077
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't have any pictures of himself.

777
00:37:13,157 --> 00:37:17,359
[SPEAKER_02]: He just had one of those cut-out post-board ones.

778
00:37:17,600 --> 00:37:18,440
[SPEAKER_00]: When he played it open?

779
00:37:19,127 --> 00:37:22,428
[SPEAKER_02]: was standing up, which was over in the mantelpiece.

780
00:37:22,448 --> 00:37:23,288
[SPEAKER_02]: That was all he had.

781
00:37:24,449 --> 00:37:26,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, he wasn't up that long, was he?

782
00:37:26,569 --> 00:37:28,830
[SPEAKER_02]: No, he played quite a few years in the Coast League, though.

783
00:37:29,490 --> 00:37:31,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, his career ran over 10 or 12 years.

784
00:37:32,891 --> 00:37:34,411
[SPEAKER_02]: But he was lost some time in the war.

785
00:37:34,451 --> 00:37:40,793
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess I had heard that he'd been gassed in the war, and this really affected his career.

786
00:37:41,433 --> 00:37:43,674
[SPEAKER_02]: And he had a couple of injuries, too, at the time.

787
00:37:43,694 --> 00:37:47,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Somebody ran into him there and broke his hand.

788
00:37:49,033 --> 00:37:54,096
[SPEAKER_02]: So he had some misfortunes, which certainly affected his career.

789
00:37:55,316 --> 00:37:59,058
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't, I don't know, he didn't seem to have too much of a sense of humor.

790
00:37:59,198 --> 00:38:00,499
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know that I expected this.

791
00:38:02,640 --> 00:38:05,381
[SPEAKER_02]: It was pretty cut and dry, the whole business.

792
00:38:06,802 --> 00:38:12,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So I didn't really have any desire to drag it out much longer.

793
00:38:13,386 --> 00:38:15,627
[SPEAKER_02]: Covered the ground as best we could in half an hour, I guess.

794
00:38:15,647 --> 00:38:17,868
[SPEAKER_02]: That's probably the longest interview he's ever had, I suppose.

795
00:38:19,047 --> 00:38:20,287
[SPEAKER_02]: That's about the shortest one I've had.

796
00:38:20,988 --> 00:38:22,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think we've covered the ground on him.

797
00:38:23,429 --> 00:38:27,470
[SPEAKER_02]: We've got about 20 minutes to go on this side.

798
00:38:31,231 --> 00:38:39,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, after we left him, it was around 12, 15 or so, we drove into Sonoma, which was Thornton Lee's hometown, whom we interviewed a couple of days ago.

799
00:38:40,895 --> 00:38:42,255
[SPEAKER_02]: No, a couple of weeks ago, I guess.

800
00:38:43,715 --> 00:38:45,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Before Palm Springs.

801
00:38:49,378 --> 00:38:53,419
[SPEAKER_02]: We found Sonoma a very attractive town, quite historic.

802
00:38:53,479 --> 00:38:58,200
[SPEAKER_02]: A plaza that looked like a real Spanish, laid-out town, much like Santa Fe had been.

803
00:38:59,340 --> 00:39:02,860
[SPEAKER_02]: These low, historic buildings circumventing the square.

804
00:39:03,661 --> 00:39:04,761
[SPEAKER_02]: Some historic buildings.

805
00:39:04,781 --> 00:39:14,722
[SPEAKER_02]: They have the monument where the Bear Flag Republic was raised back in 1846, the Announcement of the Independence of California, which was promptly repudiated by the United States.

806
00:39:15,563 --> 00:39:17,143
[SPEAKER_02]: For a brief month or so, there was a

807
00:39:18,322 --> 00:39:20,063
[SPEAKER_02]: in the independent state of California.

808
00:39:21,484 --> 00:39:22,425
[SPEAKER_02]: That's on the square.

809
00:39:23,346 --> 00:39:24,727
[SPEAKER_02]: The home of Yeho.

810
00:39:25,007 --> 00:39:27,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I never knew who he was.

811
00:39:30,111 --> 00:39:35,474
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the one who was governor, or whatever it was, when they raised the bear flag.

812
00:39:35,494 --> 00:39:39,177
[SPEAKER_02]: I see.

813
00:39:40,258 --> 00:39:43,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And the missionary is the northernmost missionary on the Mission Trail.

814
00:39:44,101 --> 00:39:45,522
[SPEAKER_00]: That would have been nice to see that missionary.

815
00:39:46,823 --> 00:39:50,485
[SPEAKER_02]: But what happened was we got a little concerned about a place to stay.

816
00:39:51,405 --> 00:39:55,987
[SPEAKER_02]: And the other interviews, I tried to call Babe Penelope and got all kinds of a run around, and I never could find her.

817
00:39:57,107 --> 00:39:57,507
[SPEAKER_02]: It was long.

818
00:39:57,527 --> 00:40:03,250
[SPEAKER_02]: About 2 o'clock, we decided to find a place to stay and then maybe see Sonora better or completely.

819
00:40:03,270 --> 00:40:04,690
[SPEAKER_02]: They had a winery we wanted to go through.

820
00:40:05,431 --> 00:40:08,952
[SPEAKER_02]: But the one motel in town was taken.

821
00:40:09,012 --> 00:40:09,913
[SPEAKER_02]: So we drove over here.

822
00:40:09,953 --> 00:40:12,774
[SPEAKER_02]: By the time we got here, we got all set up.

823
00:40:12,794 --> 00:40:14,374
[SPEAKER_02]: We just didn't feel like going back there.

824
00:40:14,394 --> 00:40:15,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything would have been closed, wouldn't it?

825
00:40:16,962 --> 00:40:20,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we might have barely made it, but it probably would have been time for a tour of the winery.

826
00:40:22,164 --> 00:40:24,025
[SPEAKER_02]: And I don't know what else we could have seen.

827
00:40:25,806 --> 00:40:31,489
[SPEAKER_02]: So we probably should have done something else, but that's the way it worked out.

828
00:40:31,529 --> 00:40:34,170
[SPEAKER_02]: We didn't get the Penelli interview.

829
00:40:34,270 --> 00:40:39,252
[SPEAKER_02]: And Mark Koenig has got his name in the phone book, but I don't know that we'll be able to see him.

830
00:40:39,272 --> 00:40:44,475
[SPEAKER_02]: We've got to get going home one of these days, and I think we're going to start tomorrow as we go farther north and west.

831
00:40:47,132 --> 00:40:49,334
[SPEAKER_02]: We keep getting farther away from home as we go home.

832
00:40:50,014 --> 00:40:51,755
[SPEAKER_02]: But we got to go farther away to start back.

833
00:40:51,876 --> 00:40:52,936
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the only way we can do it.

834
00:40:53,617 --> 00:41:08,609
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you want to cross the great, what do they call it, the great alkali desert or something.

835
00:41:10,731 --> 00:41:13,413
[SPEAKER_02]: So I guess that's about it on the Louis Guisto interview.

836
00:41:13,973 --> 00:41:15,815
[SPEAKER_02]: It wasn't the greatest interview, but I

837
00:41:16,983 --> 00:41:22,024
[SPEAKER_02]: did satisfy a desire I'd long had to meet Billy Guisto.

838
00:41:23,845 --> 00:41:24,305
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything else?

839
